    NPC: "The pantry's nearly bare. Will you forage with me tomorrow? Two pairs of eyes, twice the ground."
    YOU: "Tell me why you need this."
    NPC: "Another day like today and we're choosing who eats. Meet me at the east treeline and we'll fill packs before dark."
    YOU: Why can’t I do this now?
    NPC: As I said — Accept the forage request first. Meet at Haven at dawn on the following day.
    YOU: Why can’t I do this now?
    NPC: Like I said — Accept the forage request first. Meet at Haven at dawn on the following day.
    YOU: Why can’t I do this now?
    NPC: Still the same thing — Accept the forage request first. Meet at Haven at dawn on the following day.
    YOU: Why can’t I do this now?
    NPC: I've told you three times now — Accept the forage request first. Meet at Haven at dawn on the following day.
    YOU: Why can’t I do this now?
    NPC: We're going in circles — Accept the forage request first. Meet at Haven at dawn on the following day. Ask me something else if you want.
    YOU: "I’ll come back to help."
    NPC: "Good. The east treeline, before the dew dries. Bring your pack and your knife."
    YOU: "Hold on, back up. Then what?"
    NPC: "Can I ask you something? ... I want us to stick together. Properly. Watch each other's backs."
    YOU: "Yes. Stick together."
    NPC: "Stick together, then." They look steadier already.
    YOU: "Hold on, back up. Then what?"
    NPC: "I've got fires to light — metaphorical ones. Mostly."
    YOU: "..."
    NPC: You say nothing. Neither do they, for a while — and it's fine.
    NPC: "First light. The pantry's counting on us."
    NPC: They're smiling as you go. "Come back anytime."

